WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
THE STATE OF COMPUTERS:
Have you noticed that nobody uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:07.400
computers anymore? Ok alright, before you get mad&nbsp;
and leave a nasty comment, obviously, computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.280
are still extremely popular. Literally over a&nbsp;
billion people still own computers and hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:18.200
of millions use them everyday for work, school,&nbsp;
and entertainment. Personally, I’m very much part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.560
of this group as I’m running two computers as we&nbsp;
speak just to record this, but that’s not what I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:30.160
talking about. I’m talking about a larger macro&nbsp;
shift away from computers. Take internet traffic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:38.400
stats for example. Every month, Google gets 35.5&nbsp;
billion visits, but 77% of those visits or 27.5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.600
billion visits are from mobile. And this trend&nbsp;
holds across the board. In fact, several websites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:53.440
skew even more towards smartphones. YouTube is&nbsp;
85% smartphones, Reddit is 81%, DuckDuckGo is 90%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:00.760
Wikipedia is 80%, and certain other sites, well&nbsp;
they’re around 95%. Looking through the list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.160
you’d be pretty hard pressed to find websites&nbsp;
that are actually majority desktop. And usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.400
those are older websites that are largely&nbsp;
used by older people. For example, Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:18.800
is only 43% mobile and Microsoft Online is only&nbsp;
21% mobile. But putting aside those exceptions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.240
it’s clear that the skew is overwhelmingly towards&nbsp;
smartphones, which if you think about it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.040
sense. Nowadays, people do everything from their&nbsp;
smartphones whether it’s investing, buying a car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:35.200
buying a house, reading the news, you name it.&nbsp;
Really, the only time that most people still use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.440
computers is for productivity. You know, if your&nbsp;
job or school requires Microsoft Office or coding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.320
or 3d modeling or bunch of virtual meetings, you&nbsp;
get the idea. But putting aside productivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:53.760
people tend to do everything on their smartphones.&nbsp;
But why? Obviously, phones are more convenient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.440
on the go, but when you’re not on the go,&nbsp;
computers can accomplish a lot more. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.280
the stats suggest that unless people have to use&nbsp;
their computers, they don’t. So why in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:18.731
does nobody use computers anymore, and what does&nbsp;
this mean for the future of desktop vs mobile?

00:02:18.731 --> 00:02:20.800
THE QUANTITATIVE EXPLANATION:
Obviously this hasn’t always been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.680
case as smartphones haven’t even been around for&nbsp;
2 decades at this point, but with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:32.800
that also means that it hasn’t taken long for&nbsp;
this trend to take hold, so what happened? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:39.040
I think this trend can largely be explained by 3&nbsp;
overarching factors starting with demographics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.080
You see, this trend has less to do with computer&nbsp;
users suddenly using their smartphones more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.560
more to do with younger people just never using&nbsp;
computers. I mean, just look at the stats. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.080
average person spends 3 hours and 15 minutes&nbsp;
everyday on their smartphones which is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.760
a lot of time. Millennials aren’t too much worse&nbsp;
coming in slightly higher at 3.7 hours per day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.760
But, do you wanna guess how much time Gen Z&nbsp;
spends on their smartphones everyday? Maybe 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:12.920
5, 6 hours? Well, it’s actually up to 9 hours.&nbsp;
A lot of these people have rigorous full time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:18.240
jobs and they still have insane amounts of&nbsp;
screentime. Basically, if they’re not actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.320
working on something that requires a computer&nbsp;
or sleeping, they’re on their phones. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:28.800
you could go down the whole rabbit whole of how&nbsp;
healthy is this but that’s outside the scope of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:34.280
this video. What we’re really concerned about is&nbsp;
how this affects smartphone usage, and clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:40.280
it affects it enormously. The average person only&nbsp;
uses their computer for 2 hours and 51 minutes per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:40.874
day. So when a single zoomer clocks in over 3&nbsp;
times that on just their phones, it’s no wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.874 --> 00:03:43.400
why the stats are skewed towards smartphones.&nbsp;
But that’s just the first reason, which brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.560
us into the vastly different context of mobile&nbsp;
and desktop usage. When you use computers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.800
you naturally tend to be working on something&nbsp;
more intense that requires singular focus. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:58.560
even though you might end up spending hours on&nbsp;
a computer in any given session, it’s likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.680
that you only visited a handful of websites&nbsp;
and programs. If you’re a coder for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.360
even if you spent 10 hours in front of the screen,&nbsp;
it’s likely that your entire screen time only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:14.240
consisted of Slack, Visual Studio, Zoom, Stack&nbsp;
Overflow, and Gmail. In contrast, if you spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.800
10 hours in front of a smartphone, it’s likely&nbsp;
that your screen time was far more diverse. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:24.920
you might’ve only visited offerings from Google,&nbsp;
Meta, and Amazon, but the number of individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.600
pages you visited would likely be in the hundreds&nbsp;
if not over a 1000. If you’re scrolling through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.480
TikTok for example, you’re essentially visiting&nbsp;
a new page every 5 seconds when you swipe. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.720
if you’re just surfing the web, you’re much more&nbsp;
likely to bounce around. In fact, the average time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:46.080
spent on a desktop site is 40% longer than the&nbsp;
average time spent on a mobile site. So, again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.880
considering that, it’s no surprise why internet&nbsp;
traffic heavily leans in the favor of mobile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.800
But all of that really only applies to western&nbsp;
countries which brings us into the 3rd reason:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.360
the developing world. It’s only in western&nbsp;
countries where almost everyone has access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.200
to both a smartphone and a computer and it’s up&nbsp;
to their mood as to what they use. When we look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:11.720
at the developing world though, people can often&nbsp;
only afford one or the other, and when put in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:17.480
situation, they overwhelmingly choose to go with a&nbsp;
smartphone. In terms of numbers, there are roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:23.560
1.4 billion Windows PCs worldwide. If you throw&nbsp;
in Mac and Linux onto that, maybe that number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:30.160
goes up to 1.7 or 1.8 billion. But, do you wanna&nbsp;
guess how many smartphones are out there? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:39.360
answer is 6.94 billion or 85% of the entire world&nbsp;
population. Yeah, I don’t think that requires any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:45.560
additional explanation. So essentially, internet&nbsp;
traffic is dominated by smartphones due Gen Z,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:50.560
smartphone users bouncing around more often,&nbsp;
and the developing world at large. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:59.480
all of that only tells the quantitative side of&nbsp;
the story which still leaves the question of why.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:04.800
THE QUALITATIVE EXPLANATION:
Here’s the thing, given the data we’ve discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.480
it’s obvious that Gen Z and the developing&nbsp;
world are skewing the data towards smartphones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:17.880
but there’s more to this story because I don’t&nbsp;
think that that’s a forced reality. Virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:23.560
every Zoomer has access to both a smartphone and&nbsp;
a computer, so the fact that they spend 9 hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:30.000
a day on smartphones is very much a choice. Also,&nbsp;
regarding the developing world, even if you gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.520
them all computers right now, I suspect that their&nbsp;
use would still primarily skew towards smartphones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:41.800
for which I’ve got an explanation which primarily&nbsp;
centers on the vastly different adoptions curves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:46.120
of the computer and the smartphone. The&nbsp;
traditional computer can be traced all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:52.000
way back to 1822, but the average person didn’t&nbsp;
get a personal computer till the 1990s and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.240
the 2000s. You could say that this was because&nbsp;
computers were simply too expensive before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:04.480
90s but you could say the same things about a car.&nbsp;
Cars are super expensive but the average person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:10.320
has been getting cars since the Model T days&nbsp;
because the need outweighs the high price. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:15.880
computers, however, that was simply not the case&nbsp;
because computers were not initially designed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:21.320
average people. It was designed for corporations,&nbsp;
governments, and universities to conduct research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:27.320
do mathematical analyses, and create communication&nbsp;
and storage infrastructure. The average person in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:33.240
the 70s and 80s had no use for any of that, so&nbsp;
buying a computer for personal use was more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.280
a novelty than anything. The fact that computers&nbsp;
eventually became applicable to the average person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:44.040
was more of a byproduct of innovation as opposed&nbsp;
to the focus of innovation. With smartphones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:49.840
however, it was the exact opposite. At most, you&nbsp;
can say that the first smartphone was created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:57.360
in 1992 but smartphones really had no utility in&nbsp;
society for businesses or people until the iPhone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.440
And as for the iPhone, well, that was designed&nbsp;
specifically for the consumer. Everything from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:09.080
over the top animations and intuitive gestures to&nbsp;
the seemingly infinite app store and inviting UI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:14.440
it was all designed as a luxury purchase for&nbsp;
the average person. And this difference has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:19.720
set the stage for everything that came about&nbsp;
on both platforms. And you don’t even have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:24.560
look that far to spot these differences. Take&nbsp;
Google for instance, something that came out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:30.880
during the computer era. Originally, Google was&nbsp;
a Stanford research project meant to help people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.880
research. It just so happened that everyday people&nbsp;
eventually found utility in consulting Google on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:44.400
a regular basis. Now, in contrast, take a look at&nbsp;
mobile first services like Uber, AirBnB, SnapChat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.320
Instagram, TikTok, Robinhood, DoorDash, and so on.&nbsp;
Each and every one of these services were designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:55.760
from the ground up to maximize on consumption from&nbsp;
all fronts whether that’s screen time, engagement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:01.280
revenue, market share, you name it. So, from&nbsp;
that perspective, it’s really not surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.640
why people prefer to spend a lot more time on a&nbsp;
device that was created for consumption as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:13.400
to a device that was created for utility. And this&nbsp;
association has been engraved in people’s minds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.200
That’s probably why you tend to use your laptop&nbsp;
at your desk but your phone or your tablet on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:24.320
bed or couch. The bottomline is that people use&nbsp;
computers because it’s a utility that they need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:29.560
while they use smartphones because it’s a luxury&nbsp;
that they want. Now of course, you can argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:35.000
in the modern age, smartphones are very much also&nbsp;
a need and I completely agree with that. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:40.160
I would also argue that want still plays a far&nbsp;
larger role with smartphones than it does with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:45.440
computers. That’s why people are far more likely&nbsp;
to purchase a flagship smartphone as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:51.080
a flagship computer, even if prices were the&nbsp;
same. So, that kind of explains why nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:56.920
uses computers anymore both from a quantitative&nbsp;
standpoint and a qualitative standpoint. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.469
that leaves just one question: what does this mean&nbsp;
for the future of the desktop and mobile markets?

00:10:01.469 --> 00:10:08.080
DESKTOP VS MOBILE:&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.080
Corporations are well aware of this trend and&nbsp;
that’s why they’ve shifted their entire focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.600
towards smartphones. This includes building&nbsp;
better mobile websites, better mobile apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:25.400
and of course, spending a lot more on mobile ads.&nbsp;
I mean just take a look at this chart. In 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:30.520
desktop ad spend stood at $32 billion while&nbsp;
mobile ad spend stood at just $10 billion or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:37.880
third of desktop ad spend. By 2015 though, they&nbsp;
were both at roughly $30 billion and by 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:43.840
mobile had grown to $65 billion while desktop was&nbsp;
down to $25 billion. And as you would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:49.600
guess, this disparity has only grown evern&nbsp;
further thanks to the pandemic. As of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.760
mobile accounts for 70% of programmatic digital&nbsp;
ad spend within the US while desktops and laptops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:01.880
only account for a mere 13%. Does this mean that&nbsp;
computers are just dead and companies will stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:07.840
offering desktop platforms? No, of course not.&nbsp;
While people prefer to spend the majority of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:14.000
time on mobile devices, they still very much value&nbsp;
having a desktop option as well. For example, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.760
banking, I probably only log on to the desktop&nbsp;
portal a few times a year while I log in to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:24.600
mobile portal almost every day. But, I still very&nbsp;
much value the desktop portal because it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:30.240
downloading account statements and filing taxes a&nbsp;
lot easier. Similarly, if I’ve watching Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:36.720
it’s always on my iPad or TV, but if I were to&nbsp;
manage my membership or cancel my subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:42.960
I’d probably do it on the computer. And I get&nbsp;
the feeling that this is how most people feel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.080
let me know down below if you agree. With that&nbsp;
being said, I suspect that services will become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:52.600
more and more mobile centered while desktop&nbsp;
offerings become more and more geared towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:57.520
managing these services or doing tasks that&nbsp;
aren’t as easy on mobile. Desktops will also of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:03.080
course continue to be extremely relevant when&nbsp;
it comes to productivity, but that’s why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:08.880
internet is now dominated by smartphones. Another&nbsp;
industry that has seen an extremely interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:14.160
evolution is TVs which seem to infinitely get&nbsp;
cheaper. Check out this video to learn more.

